   Well, after playing some Crawl recently, I came to believe that the
reason NetHack's mid- and late-game are such no brainers is the
resistances system. Eat shrieker or cave spider and whole class of
dangerous situations are solved in one fell swoop, get GDSM and many
powerful magical attacks are no longer any threat, etc. This seems
really ridiculous. Most players will be completely immune by the time
they reach the Castle and many even before that. With high AC, which
is also not hard to come by, the player are near invincible.
   I like how Crawl solves this: resistances are much harder to come
by:

1) They can never be gained by eating corpses
2) Some can be gained temporarily by quaffing a potion of resistance
3) Some can be gained by playing particular species (like nagas'
poison resistance)
4) Many can be gained by mutations, but this is very dangerous way, as
mutations are for the most part random and you can gain bad ones as
well
5) Most resistances have three levels and those that do don't grant
perfect immunity even at the highest level (poison resistance is still
binary have/have not property and grants complete immunity, though)
6) Most common way of getting resistant is wearing artifacts -- this
is far from no brainer, though: you only have that many slots to fill,
so you usually won't be able to get completely resistant; also, many
of the artifacts bundle their resistances with disadvantageous
properties (like reducing your strength, intelligence, other
resistances etc.)

   These points make resistances major game-play issue -- a striking
contrast to NetHack's "get all of them, bother no more" system.

